@page "/tv"
@implements IDisposable
@inject CatalogService Catalog
@inject Announcer Announcer
@inject IJSRuntime Js

@* The series list. The same wall as the films page, over shows.json rather than movies.json, and with no
   facet panel: 122 series is three pages, and a filter over them would be a second copy of Movies.razor's
   query state for a list a reader can take in by scrolling.

   The PageTitle and the h1 are in the first render and outside every state branch: FocusOnNavigate fires
   once after the first render and never retries, and shows.json arrives long after that. *@
<PageTitle>TV - Film and TV library</PageTitle>

@* "TV" and not "TV series" or "Television": SC 2.4.6 wants a heading that describes the content, and
   SC 2.4.4 and SC 3.2.4 want the nav link that leads here to say the same thing. NavMenu holds the link's
   word and MarkupContractTests pins the two against each other, so the pair cannot drift.

   tabindex="-1" is not for FocusOnNavigate, which adds it itself. It is for ElementReference.FocusAsync(),
   which does NOT add it and on an element that cannot take focus fails silently -- no exception, no moved
   focus, no failing test. The successful-retry transition below depends on this attribute, so it is a
   feature dependency here and not a conformance claim. *@
<h1 tabindex="-1" @ref="heading">TV</h1>

@* ONE stable container, not three sibling branches that swap nodes: replacing the node would destroy the
   node a screen reader's virtual cursor may be sitting on. The paragraph is the same element in every
   state and only its text changes; the button is the only thing that comes and goes.

   No live region of any kind in here: no alert role, no status role, no log role, and no live-region
   attribute. (Spelled in words on purpose: SourceContractTests scans these files for the literal markers,
   and a comment quoting one fails exactly as the attribute would.) The announcer is the only speaking
   channel, and the deterministic channel for the failure is the focus move, not a region. SC 4.1.3 Status
   Messages, Level AA, applies only to messages that do NOT receive focus. *@
<div class="tv-state" tabindex="-1" @ref="stateContainer">
    <p>@Sentence</p>

    @if (ShowTryAgain)
    {
        @* aria-disabled, never the disabled ATTRIBUTE: disabling the focused element blurs it and drops
           focus to <body>. The click still arrives and is ignored in TryAgain instead. *@
        <button type="button" class="try-again" aria-disabled="@(retrying ? "true" : null)" @onclick="TryAgain">Try again</button>
    }
</div>

@* THE WALL, AND ONLY IN THE READY STATE. It sits BELOW the state container, so the reading order is the
   heading, then the one-sentence state, then the count, then the pager, then the tiles.

   NO SORT HERE. The build writes shows.json in the order the owner's sheet puts the series in, and a page
   that re-sorted would disagree with the file for no reason a reader could see. The films page does the
   same with movies.json.

   PAGE SIZE IS NOT PASSED. The grid's default is 60, which makes three pages of the measured 122 series,
   and a second copy of the number here would be one edit away from disagreeing with the first.

   A METHOD GROUP AND NOT A LAMBDA for KeyOf, for the reason Movies.razor records: a lambda would put a >
   inside an attribute value, and SourceContractTests reads this tag with a regex that stops at the first
   one. It also says in one place what identifies a series, which is the slug the build wrote. *@
@if (HasSeries)
{
    <PosterGrid TItem="ShowSummary" Items="shows.Value" KeyOf="PublishedSlug"
                Page="page" PageChanged="OnPageChanged" Context="show">
        <PosterTile Tile="TileView.ForShow(show)" />
    </PosterGrid>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The failure sentence. ONE constant for the visible paragraph and for the <c>Alert</c>, so the seen
    /// and the spoken copies cannot drift apart. Internal so a test can assert they are the same string
    /// rather than two literals that happen to match today.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string CouldNotLoad =
        "The TV library could not be loaded. Check your connection and try again.";

    /// <summary>The visible loading copy, with the ellipsis CHARACTER rather than three full stops.</summary>
    internal const string LoadingVisible = "Loading series…";

    /// <summary>
    /// The spoken loading copy, without the ellipsis: a screen reader either reads the character aloud or
    /// swallows it, and neither is what the sentence means.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string LoadingSpoken = "Loading series";

    /// <summary>The empty-library sentence.</summary>
    internal const string EmptyLibrary = "The library holds no series.";

    private ElementReference heading;
    private ElementReference stateContainer;

    private CatalogResult<IReadOnlyList<ShowSummary>> shows = new(CatalogState.Loading, []);

    private bool disposed;
    private bool retrying;
    private bool loadingAnnounced;
    private bool failureReported;
    private bool movedFocusToFailure;
    private bool movingFocus;
    private bool focusHeadingAfterRetry;

    private int page = 1;

    /// <summary>The slug the build wrote, which is also the only string <c>GetShowAsync</c> will accept.</summary>
    private static string PublishedSlug(ShowSummary show) => show.Slug;

    private bool HasSeries => shows.State == CatalogState.Ready && shows.Value.Count > 0;

    /// <remarks>Shown while the fetch is in flight too, so a retry that is still running keeps its button.</remarks>
    private bool ShowTryAgain => shows.State == CatalogState.Failed || retrying;

    /// <remarks>
    /// "series" is its own plural, so unlike the films page this has no singular form to get wrong -- and
    /// the count is culture-formatted for the same reason that one's is.
    /// </remarks>
    private string Sentence => shows.State switch
    {
        CatalogState.Failed => CouldNotLoad,
        CatalogState.Loading => LoadingVisible,
        _ => shows.Value.Count switch
        {
            0 => EmptyLibrary,
            var n => $"The library holds {n.ToString("N0")} series.",
        },
    };

    /// <remarks>
    /// Nothing is announced here. A message raised before the first render starts its settle timer while
    /// the clearing render is still queued, so the real gap becomes "delay minus handler time" and
    /// collapses on exactly the slow devices the gap exists for. Everything this page says it says from
    /// <see cref="OnAfterRenderAsync"/>.
    /// <para>
    /// The fetch is awaited and never cancelled, here or on dispose. The task is shared and cached by the
    /// service, so cancelling it would turn one reader's navigation away into a Failed result for the next
    /// visit to this page.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        var result = await Catalog.GetShowsAsync();
        if (disposed) return;

        shows = result;
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// <b>NOTHING IS ANNOUNCED ON A SUCCESSFUL LOAD, and that is a decision rather than an omission.</b>
    /// RouteAnnouncer already says "TV" on arrival and the count is visible text in the container above, so
    /// a status message would be a second utterance for an arrival the reader has already been told about.
    /// The films page speaks its count because a FILTER changes it under the reader without any other
    /// signal; there is no filter here, so the only things worth speaking are the three below.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (disposed) return;

        switch (shows.State)
        {
            case CatalogState.Loading:
                // Only because the page is STILL loading after a render, and at most once a visit. On a
                // return visit the catalogue is cached, the task is already complete, ComponentBase
                // produces no intermediate render and the page is Ready on the first render -- so this
                // never runs, and nothing is ever said about a page that had already loaded.
                if (!loadingAnnounced)
                {
                    loadingAnnounced = true;
                    Announcer.Status(LoadingSpoken);
                }

                break;

            case CatalogState.Failed:
                // This page armed the polite region with the loading line, so this page clears it. The
                // failure is carried by the focus move or the alert below, and a region still saying
                // "Loading series" beside a page that says the library could not be loaded is a stale
                // statement. Idempotent over an already-empty region.
                Announcer.Status("");

                // THE FOCUS MOVE IS THE PRIMARY CHANNEL and it runs FIRST, because a screen reader reads
                // the contents of a focused unnamed tabindex="-1" container and this container's whole text
                // IS the failure sentence. A move that happens therefore already says it, in the words on
                // screen, and announcing the text of whatever focus has just moved to is forbidden.
                // The flag is set only when the move actually happened, so a reader who had the drawer open
                // when the fetch failed is not denied the move for ever.
                var movedOnThisPass = false;
                if (!movedFocusToFailure && !movingFocus)
                {
                    movingFocus = true;
                    try { movedOnThisPass = movedFocusToFailure = await PageFocus.IfUnmoved(Js, stateContainer); }
                    finally { movingFocus = false; }
                }

                // One report for one failure the reader can see, and THE ALERT IS THE FALLBACK -- raised
                // only where the focus move did not happen. Raising both speaks the sentence twice: once as
                // the contents of the container focus landed on, and again a settle delay later when the
                // assertive region interrupts with the identical text.
                // On a FAILED RETRY focus is usually already on the Try again button, which is not a
                // keeper, so the helper refuses, nothing moves, and this fires -- which is how a reader who
                // retries and fails again is not met with silence.
                // movingFocus is in the condition because a render can arrive while the first pass's focus
                // call is suspended at its await: that pass has not chosen a channel yet, and this one must
                // not choose for it.
                if (!failureReported && !movingFocus)
                {
                    failureReported = true;
                    if (!movedOnThisPass) Announcer.Alert(CouldNotLoad);
                }

                break;

            case CatalogState.Ready:
                // The successful retry, and the only focus move on a working page. The button the reader
                // was standing on has gone with the failed state, so focus would otherwise drop to <body>.
                // Landing on the h1 announces "TV, heading level 1", which is how the reader learns the
                // retry worked without a second assertive message saying so.
                if (focusHeadingAfterRetry)
                {
                    focusHeadingAfterRetry = false;
                    await PageFocus.Outright(heading);
                }

                break;
        }
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>GetShowsAsync</c> drops a failed task of its own accord, so a second call really does fetch
    /// again rather than handing back the cached failure.
    /// </remarks>
    private async Task TryAgain()
    {
        // The button carries aria-disabled rather than the disabled attribute, so the click still arrives
        // while a retry is in flight and has to be ignored here.
        if (retrying) return;

        // The failure has ended as far as the reader is concerned. A cancellation, not a message -- it
        // takes effect at once and speaks nothing. The next Alert is raised from OnAfterRenderAsync and
        // never in this handler: on a device slow enough to flush both mutations in one frame the second
        // call would see the region already empty, mark the clear observed, and the repeated identical
        // sentence would reach the DOM as no change at all.
        Announcer.Alert("");

        retrying = true;
        failureReported = false;
        movedFocusToFailure = false;
        shows = new(CatalogState.Loading, shows.Value);   // the loading copy, visibly

        var result = await Catalog.GetShowsAsync();
        if (disposed) return;

        retrying = false;
        shows = result;
        focusHeadingAfterRetry = result.State == CatalogState.Ready;
    }

    /// <summary>Adopts the page the grid asked for, and says nothing.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The grid announces the change itself: whoever writes a standing statement is whoever clears it, and
    /// the grid is what clears the region on its own dispose. Announcing here as well would speak the page
    /// twice -- and would speak it on the FIRST render, because the grid raises this for an out-of-range
    /// correction as well as for a button press.
    /// <para>
    /// The number is adopted EXACTLY as given. The grid builds its announcement from it, and a page that
    /// clamped, ignored or overrode it would have the grid state a page the reader is not on. Unlike the
    /// films page it is NOT written to the address: there is no query state on this route yet, so a deep
    /// link to a page of series is a thing this page cannot offer and does not pretend to.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private void OnPageChanged(int value) => page = value;

    /// <remarks>
    /// Clear what this page set, and raise nothing new. A cancellation -- it takes effect at once and
    /// speaks nothing. Without it "Loading series" sits in #status-announcer, readable in browse mode, long
    /// after the reader has left, because the announcer is a scoped singleton and MainLayout survives
    /// navigation.
    /// <para>
    /// #route-announcer is NOT cleared here, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. RouteAnnouncer
    /// is its only caller -- SourceContractTests pins that -- and it writes the region empty on the way in
    /// to this page and the next page's name on the way out. A clear from here would land after that write
    /// on any navigation where this component is disposed last, and the latest-wins rule would drop the
    /// name of the page the reader just arrived at.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        disposed = true;
        Announcer.Status("");
        Announcer.Alert("");
    }
}
